{"7/subjects/mathematics/chapters/arithmetic_expressions/topics/2_2/questions/q017":{"id":"q017","type":"MCQ","text":"Among 84 − 38 − 12, 84 − (37+12), 83 − 38 − 13, and −37 + 83 − 12, which one is NOT equal to 83 − 37 − 12?","options":["84 − 38 − 12","−37 + 83 − 12","83 − 38 − 13","83 + (−37) + (−12)"],"answer":"C","conceptTags":["Rewriting subtraction as addition of a negative term","Terms of an expression are separated by a plus sign"],"difficulty":"VeryHard","marks":1,"imageRequired":false,"imageUrls":[],"board":"CBSE","class":7,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"arithmetic_expressions","topic":"Brackets and Terms in Expressions","source":"manual","version":2,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-v6-audit","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["explanation","wrongOptionReasons"],"at":1789991078310,"by":"claude-task-d-explanations","note":"Task D: merged explanation rewrite for arithmetic_expressions, grounded in gegp102.pdf"},"explanation":"83 − 38 − 13 = 32, which is not equal to 83 − 37 − 12 = 34. '84 − 38 − 12' is wrong because 84 − 38 − 12 = 34, which is equal; '−37 + 83 − 12' is wrong because −37 + 83 − 12 = 34, which is equal; and '83 + (−37) + (−12)' is wrong because 83 + (−37) + (−12) = 34, which is equal.","bloomLevel":3,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860154880,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"7/subjects/mathematics/chapters/finding_the_unknown/topics/7_1/questions/q005":{"id":"q005","type":"MCQ","text":"On a balanced weighing scale, one pan holds 6 units and the other holds e + e. Framing this as an equation gives:","options":["2e = 6","e + 6 = 2","e = 6","6e = 2"],"answer":"A","conceptTags":["Framing an equation from a situation"],"difficulty":"Easy","marks":1,"imageRequired":true,"imageUrls":["data:image/svg+xml;base64,"],"board":"CBSE","class":7,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"finding_the_unknown","topic":"Framing Equations and the Balance Idea","source":"manual","version":2,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-v6-audit","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["explanation","wrongOptionReasons"],"at":1789997625772,"by":"claude-task-d-explanations","note":"Task D: merged explanation rewrite for finding_the_unknown, grounded in gegp207.pdf"},"explanation":"One pan holds 6 units and the other holds e + e = 2e; a balanced scale means the two are equal, so 2e = 6. 'e + 6 = 2' is wrong because e + 6 = 2 mixes the contents of the two pans into one side; 'e = 6' is wrong because e = 6 counts only one e when the pan holds two; and '6e = 2' is wrong because 6e = 2 multiplies 6 by e, but the pan holds two copies of e added together.","bloomLevel":1,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860155193,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"7/subjects/mathematics/chapters/parallel_and_intersecting_lines/topics/5_1/questions/q007":{"id":"q007","type":"MCQ","text":"In a figure, two lines cross and one marked angle x sits next to an angle of 105 degrees along a straight line. The value of x is:","options":["105 degrees","95 degrees","75 degrees","85 degrees"],"answer":"C","conceptTags":["The angles of a linear pair add up to 180 degrees","Adjacent angles formed by two intersecting lines are a linear pair"],"difficulty":"Medium","marks":1,"imageRequired":true,"imageUrls":["data:image/svg+xml;base64,"],"board":"CBSE","class":7,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"parallel_and_intersecting_lines","topic":"Linear Pairs and Vertically Opposite Angles","source":"manual","version":2,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-v6-audit","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["explanation","wrongOptionReasons"],"at":1789996766191,"by":"claude-task-d-explanations","note":"Task D: merged explanation rewrite for parallel_and_intersecting_lines, grounded in gegp105.pdf"},"explanation":"x + 105 = 180, so x = 75 degrees. '105 degrees' is wrong because 105 degrees is the given angle; '95 degrees' is wrong because 95 degrees is not 180 − 105; and '85 degrees' is wrong because 85 degrees is not 180 − 105.","bloomLevel":2,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860155588,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"7/subjects/mathematics/chapters/parallel_and_intersecting_lines/topics/5_1/questions/q010":{"id":"q010","type":"MCQ","text":"In the figure, two lines cross. The four angles in order around the point are a, b, c and the marked angle x. If b = 130 degrees, then a equals:","options":["130 degrees","50 degrees","90 degrees","40 degrees"],"answer":"B","conceptTags":["The angles of a linear pair add up to 180 degrees","Vertically opposite angles are always equal"],"difficulty":"Medium","marks":1,"imageRequired":true,"imageUrls":["data:image/svg+xml;base64,"],"board":"CBSE","class":7,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"parallel_and_intersecting_lines","topic":"Linear Pairs and Vertically Opposite Angles","source":"manual","version":2,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-v6-audit","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["explanation","wrongOptionReasons"],"at":1789996766464,"by":"claude-task-d-explanations","note":"Task D: merged explanation rewrite for parallel_and_intersecting_lines, grounded in gegp105.pdf"},"explanation":"a and b are adjacent on a straight line, so a = 180 − 130 = 50 degrees. '130 degrees' is wrong because 130 degrees is b itself; '90 degrees' is wrong because a is not 90 degrees; and '40 degrees' is wrong because 40 degrees is not 180 − 130.","bloomLevel":2,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860155588,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"8/subjects/mathematics/chapters/algebra_play/topics/6_1/questions/q008":{"id":"q008","type":"MCQ","text":"Mukta later reports 1390 for the same trick. A student computes 1390 - 165 and reads off the date:","options":["1st March","13th September","39th of month 12","25th December, since 1390 - 165 = 1225"],"answer":"D","conceptTags":["Reversing the steps to recover the input"],"difficulty":"Hard","marks":1,"imageRequired":false,"imageUrls":[],"board":"CBSE","class":8,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"algebra_play","topic":"Think of a Number Tricks","source":"manual","version":2,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-v6-audit","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["explanation","wrongOptionReasons"],"at":1789938735839,"by":"claude-task-d-explanations","note":"Task D: merged explanation rewrite for algebra_play, grounded in hegp206.pdf"},"explanation":"1390 − 165 = 1225 = 100M + D, so M = 12 and D = 25, the 25th of December. '1st March' is wrong because it would give 100 × 3 + 1 = 301; '13th September' is wrong because it would give 900 + 13 = 913; and '39th of month 12' is wrong because 1200 + 39 = 1239, not 1225, and there is no 39th day.","bloomLevel":3,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860161332,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"8/subjects/mathematics/chapters/we_distribute_yet_things_multiply/topics/6_2/questions/s013":{"id":"s013","type":"LA","text":"(a) Expand both (a+b)² and (a-b)² using the given values. (3)\n(b) Find the remaining area (garden minus flower bed) and simplify using the identity connecting the two expansions if helpful. (2)","scenario":"A square garden of side (a+b) metres has a smaller square flower bed of side (a-b) metres cut from one corner, and a student wants to express the remaining area using both squaring identities, given a=15, b=5.","modelAnswer":"(a) (a+b)² = 15+5=20, 20²=400 sq m (garden area). (a-b)² = 15-5=10, 10²=100 sq m (flower bed area). (b) Remaining area = 400-100 = 300 sq m; note this remaining area equals (a+b)²-(a-b)², which algebraically simplifies to 4ab = 4(15)(5) = 300 sq m, matching and offering a useful cross-check shortcut.","acceptableAnswers":["garden = 400 sq m, flower bed = 100 sq m","remaining = 300 sq m, matching 4ab=300"],"answerFormat":"paragraph","diagramExpected":false,"unitsRequired":false,"rubric":{"mode":"fixed_steps","totalMarks":5,"mustShowWorking":false,"steps":[{"step":1,"what":"Correctly computes both areas (400 and 100 sq m)","marks":3},{"step":2,"what":"Correctly finds the remaining area as 300 sq m","marks":2}]},"conceptTags":["The two square-of-a-sum and square-of-a-difference identities give 2(a squared plus b squared) equals (a plus b) squared plus (a minus b) squared"],"difficulty":"VeryHard","marks":5,"board":"CBSE","class":8,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"we_distribute_yet_things_multiply","topic":"Squaring Sums and Differences","source":"manual","version":1,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-subjective-authoring","contentModifiedSinceReview":{"fields":["imageUrls"],"at":1789850805812,"by":"claude-task-c-images","note":"square garden of side (a+b) with a square bed of side (a-b); remaining area left as ?"},"imageUrls":["data:image/svg+xml;base64,"],"imageRequired":true,"bloomLevel":5,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860161950,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"7/subjects/mathematics/chapters/parallel_and_intersecting_lines/topics/5_2/questions/s014":{"id":"s014","type":"LA","text":"(a) Explain why the Pythagorean relationship can be validly applied here, connecting it to the fact that adjacent walls of the rectangular room are perpendicular (forming a right angle at each corner). (2)\n(b) Calculate the exact length of the diagonal beam using the Pythagorean relationship. (3)","scenario":"A student is given a rectangular room and told that a diagonal support beam is installed from one corner to the OPPOSITE corner, and wants to determine, using the Pythagorean relationship (since the room's adjacent walls are perpendicular), the exact length of this beam for a room 6m by 8m, connecting the perpendicularity of the walls to why Pythagoras applies here at all.","modelAnswer":"(a) The Pythagorean relationship can be validly applied here because it specifically requires a RIGHT-ANGLED triangle, and since the two adjacent walls of the rectangular room are perpendicular (meeting at exactly 90 degrees at each corner), the triangle formed by the two walls (length 8m, width 6m) and the diagonal beam is indeed a genuine right-angled triangle, with the right angle located exactly at the corner where the two walls meet — this perpendicularity is the precise condition that makes Pythagoras's theorem applicable. (b) Diagonal² = 6² + 8² = 36+64=100, so diagonal = √100 = 10 metres.","acceptableAnswers":["perpendicular walls create the right angle needed for Pythagoras to apply","diagonal = 10 metres"],"answerFormat":"paragraph","diagramExpected":false,"unitsRequired":false,"rubric":{"mode":"fixed_steps","totalMarks":5,"mustShowWorking":false,"steps":[{"step":1,"what":"Correctly explains why the perpendicular walls justify using Pythagoras's theorem here","marks":2},{"step":2,"what":"Correctly calculates the diagonal length as 10 metres","marks":3}]},"conceptTags":["The adjacent edges of a rectangular sheet are perpendicular","A right angle measures 90 degrees"],"difficulty":"VeryHard","marks":5,"imageRequired":false,"imageUrls":[],"board":"CBSE","class":7,"subject":"Mathematics","chapterRef":"parallel_and_intersecting_lines","topic":"Perpendicular Lines","source":"manual","version":1,"humanReview":false,"lastEditedBy":"claude-subjective-authoring","bloomLevel":5,"publishMigration":"tulna-readiness-2026-10-01","publishedAt":1790860155588,"status":"published","bank":"own"},"questionBank/class_6/publishers/board_practice_bank/subjects/mathematics/chapters/data_handling_and_presentation/topics/4_3/questions/cbq_q007":{"status":"published"},"questionBank/class_6/publishers/board_practice_bank/subjects/mathematics/chapters/data_handling_and_presentation/topics/4_3/questions/cbq_q008":{"status":"published"},"questionBank/class_6/publishers/board_practice_bank/subjects/mathematics/chapters/data_handling_and_presentation/topics/4_3/questions/cbq_q009":{"status":"published"},"questionBank/class_6/publishers/board_practice_bank/subjects/mathematics/chapter